## Permission-mode regression — workspace-write tools and ASK allow shortcut — 2026-11-11

User report: with `workspace-write`, the model appeared unable to use tools; asked whether the ASK-mode `a` shortcut works as Allow.

Root cause found:
- The permission layer correctly normalizes `workspace-write` to `ModeWorkspace`, permits structured workspace tools, and restricts shell execution separately.
- Tool exposure is also intersected with the active role's `allowedTools`.
- Built-in role files are seeded only when missing and are intentionally never overwritten. A legacy built-in role JSON file that predates `allowedTools` therefore loaded as a nil/empty allowlist. `workspace-write` then advertised no tools because it respects role allowlists; `autopilot` appeared to fix it because unrestricted mode bypasses the role allowlist.
- Recovery: when a built-in role file omits the `allowedTools` key, use that built-in role's shipped allowlist in memory. Do not rewrite the user's file. Preserve an explicit `allowedTools: []` as an intentional no-tools policy. Custom roles with the field omitted remain fail-closed.

ASK shortcut review:
- Raw TUI input maps `a` / `A` to `RUNE:a` / `RUNE:A`.
- Normal ASK approvals map `a` to `ApprovalOnce` plus response-scoped allow; it allows the pending operation and remaining operations in the current response.
- Lifecycle/system actions remain one-time only and must not accept response-wide `a`; they require key `1`.
- Refactored shortcut selection into a testable helper and added input/mapping regression tests. Web UI retains its explicit “Allow this response” button.

Regression coverage added:
- Legacy built-in default role with omitted allowlist regains shipped tools under workspace-write.
- Explicit empty built-in allowlist remains empty.
- Agent advertises workspace-write tools and can execute a workspace file write using the migrated legacy default role.
- TUI a/A maps to response-wide allow; lifecycle approval rejects a and accepts only one-time key 1.

Verification pending: run `gofmt`, `go test ./...`, `go vet ./...`, shell/completion tests, and Linux/amd64 static build in GitHub Actions. Do not claim the user-specific machine configuration is confirmed; the code fix addresses the legacy built-in-role migration path.


